Page=new Array();Page[0]=new Array("Assisted Strategy Design provides a controlled environment for the design, review and improvement of decisioning strategy trees, i.e. of segmentation and assignments.","Each Assisted Design Project centres around an Analysis Data Set that contains all characteristics used in the existing strategy, as well as one or more Target Characteristics, any number of Predictor Characteristics and other characteristics required for use in Measures. The Analysis Data Set is an embedded or by reference Input Data Connection.","In addition to the primary Analysis Data Set, the user can define an optional comparison data set. This enables the use of one data set to develop the segmentation and outcome assignment, for example, and another to validate the design. These can be physically distinct data files, or different sub-sets of the same physical data set as specified by a data set filter. ","Measures based on key performance indicators, related to the Target Characteristic, Predictor Characteristics and other characteristics in the Analysis Data Set or generated as a result of strategy execution, can be included in the Assisted Design Project to help the user validate the strategy as well as to direct strategy design/tree growing.","Additionally, the user can create Impact measures to perform impact analysis and to drive the automatic assignment of best outcomes to strategy tree nodes.","Project Characteristics enable the user to create measures quickly and easily at the point they are required during the Assisted Design process - from within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, the Measures sub editor, or the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.","The user can perform Intelligent Tree Growing to grow and manipulate tree segments, using &quot;Next Best Split Analysis&quot; to interactively grow one level at a time from a given node, or &quot;Grow&quot; to automatically grow multiple levels from a given node (both approaches can use CHAID or CART.) ","The user can then manually make assignments to resultant tree nodes, or use Propose Best Outcomes to automatically select outcomes that best satisfy the user's business goals and constraints.","The user can fine tune and test the resulting segmentation and assigned strategies before promoting the tree to the System Composition, for use in a decision process flow or in a 3rd party decisioning environment. ","Assisted Strategy Design overview","AssistedStrategyDesignoverview.htm");
Page[1]=new Array("The Assisted Design Project Tree editor enables the user to create or modify a tree of the type selected when building the Assisted Design Project. Consequently, the layout of the editor depends upon the type of tree selected. However, there are several ways in which the Assisted Design Project Tree editor is unique:","By default, the Leaf Node ID, Leaf Node Name, and Component Type columns are hidden. The user can un-hide these by clicking the Column Display button @.","The Assisted Design Project Tree editor tool bar includes 11 additional buttons that enable the user to import an existing tree into the Assisted Design Project, to analyse the tree, to grow the tree, to propose best outcomes to assign, to run and review batch tests of the tree, and to promote the tree for use outside the project. The Assisted Design Project also enables the user to specify a comparison data set in the Analysis Data Set sub editor for use in checking the validity of the segmentation and outcome assignment.","Measures can be used to assess the current strategy tree as well as to direct strategy design/tree growing. These measures are calculated and displayed in columns in the Assisted Design Project Tree editor. Project Characteristics are available in the Resources window to enable the user to create embedded measures as needed from within the editor. The Compare Measures features allows the user to quickly compare measures between the primary analysis data set and the comparison data set. ","Right clicking a leaf node displays two selections that enable the user to grow a tree automatically and perform a next best split analysis, and a selection that enables the user to define constraints and eligibility and propose the best outcomes to assign given the goal and these criteria.","Any assigned outcome component can be opened from within the Outcomes table by right clicking on the component and selecting Open. ","It is possible to create and maintain Assisted Strategy Design projects based on multi-process trees (known as Decision Process Trees), as shown in the following example:","All the functionality for an Assisted Design Project on a Decision Process Tree is the same as for all other component tree types, but there are some additional characteristics available in the Project Characteristics. See Using Project Characteristics to create measures for more information.","Assisted Design Project Tree editor","AssistedDesignProjectTreeeditor.htm");
Page[2]=new Array("The Assisted Design Project Tree editor tool bar is used to assist the user in defining a tree, to alter the view of the tree seen in the editor, to analyse the tree, to grow the tree, to propose best outcomes to assign, to run and review batch tests of the tree, to import an existing tree into the Assisted Design Project, and to promote the tree for use outside the project. At any stage in the analysis, compare measures across the primary analysis data set and a chosen comparison data set.","The first six buttons are the same for all tree editor tool bars:","@ Test - launches Interactive Test for the tree within the Assisted Design Project.","@ Assign outcome - adds the outcome colour that the user has selected in the Outcome table to the chosen leaf node.","@ Clear outcome - removes the outcome colour from the selected leaf node and sets the outcome to &lt;&lt;Unassigned&gt;&gt;","@ Add bookmark - allows the user to bookmark specific split headers in the tree. When the contents of a tree are displayed in the Navigator window the bookmarks are displayed separately. Clicking on a bookmark in the Navigator window will open the tree in the Segmentation Tree editor at that point in the tree, giving the user quick access to a specific split header in the tree.","@ Add annotation - select to add annotation to any node of the tree. Once some annotation has been added, the user can choose to display it or hide all, edit, remove or show all annotations by using options selected from the right click menu accessed from any node.","@ Table View - select to display only the leaf nodes of the tree. This view has been designed to make it easier for the user to assign outcomes. (Tree growing operations are not permitted in this view)","The last eleven buttons are unique to the Assisted Design Project Tree editor tool bar:","@ Calculate Measures Against Tree Scenario - select to calculate the selected measures for the tree and display the results.","@ Find Next Best Split for the selected node - select to perform Next Best Split analysis to identify and rank a set of predictor characteristics for the next level of the tree.","@ Grow the tree from the selected node - select to allow Assisted Design to grow the tree one or more levels automatically.","@ Propose the best outcomes to assign to the selected node(s) - select to perform Propose Best Outcomes for selected nodes of the tree.","@ Perform Batch Test - select to perform a batch test on this tree.","@ Show Batch Test Results - select to display the batch test results. ","@ Import an existing tree - select to import an existing tree from the system composition to replace the existing tree scenario in this Assisted Design Project.","@ Save this scenario as new tree - select to save the tree in its current saved state as a tree component for shared use within the domain.","@ Replace original tree with this scenario - select to replace the tree that was used as the basis for this Assisted Design Project with the tree in its current saved state.","@ Merge with Original Tree - select to merge this tree branch in its current saved state with the tree that was used as the basis for this Assisted Design Project.","@ Promote as Challenger in the original Decision Process Flow - select to promote this tree scenario as a challenger to the tree that was used as the basis for this Assisted Design Project, within the originator decision process flow.","@ Enter measure comparison mode - select to compare measures between the primary data set and comparison data set.","Assisted Design Project Tree editor tool bar","AssistedDesignProjectTreeeditortoolbar.htm");
Page[3]=new Array("The Analysis Data Set sub editor enables the user to specify the data source for use in building and validating the Assisted Design Project. The Analysis Data Set is an embedded or by reference Input Data Connection. Using a by reference Input Data Connection enables a common definition of input data set and mappings to be shared by one or more Assisted Design Projects and/or Test Projects.","The user can apply a filter to the Analysis Data Set (e.g, include only Accepted Applications) to limit the records included in the analysis.","The user can define a primary data and an optional comparison data set (e.g, a validation data set). These can be physically distinct data files, or different sub-sets of the same physical data set as specified by a data set filter.","The Analysis Data set sub editor can be activated by double clicking on Analysis Data Set in the Navigator window, or by right clicking on the sub editor and selecting Open Editor. The sub editor will open in the main editor window.","Embedded and by reference Input Data Connections and filters can be specified by dragging and dropping from the Palette and Resources windows:","See Specify an embedded Input Data Connection or Specify a by-reference Input Data Connection for more information.  ","See the Input Data Connection help for information on creating and modifying the Input Data Connection.","Analysis Data Set sub editor","AnalysisDataSetsubeditor.htm");
Page[4]=new Array("The Measures sub editor allows the user to create referenced and embedded measures based on any characteristic in the Analysis Data Set, or produced by the tree or assigned impact scripts. The user can also create embedded measures using Project Characteristics. These measures can be used to assess the current strategy tree as well as to direct strategy design/tree growing. The measures are calculated and displayed in columns in the Assisted Design Project Tree editor. ","Typically, the user would define and configure measures to help assess the population (i.e., the whole tree and the key segments therein) in terms of Key Performance Indicators (Measures) based on known performance outcome characteristics &#8211; for example, measures based on known Delinquency Status, Total Outstanding Balance, Current Credit Limit or Credit Limit Utilisation.","These outcome characteristics would typically be related to the Target Characteristic(s) used to grow the tree segmentation and assign strategies to the resultant nodes.","Typical measures could include:","# Good Customers in the tree and each of its segments","# Bad Customers in the tree and each of its segments","Total / Average Outstanding Balance in the tree and each of its segments","Average Credit Limit Utilisation in the tree and each of its segments","Most measures inside assisted design will be input measures; i.e., measures based on characteristics in the Analysis Data Set.","To better enable Intelligent Tree Growing and to assist in the identification of Predictor Characteristics, the user can define and configure measures based on candidate predictors &#8211; for example, Min, Max, Total, Average, Std Deviation of Disposable Income.","Informed by measures, the user can select a node within the tree to work on: for example; a low risk score band exhibiting a higher than expected delinquency rate, with significant population size.","Results measures are used to assess the impact of the tree&#8217;s assignments - either existing assignments or assignments made to newly added leaf nodes - and to drive and assess the Propose Best Outcomes analytical process.","Results measures are based on results characteristics.  Results measures are produced either by the component assigned to the tree&#8217;s outcomes or by the impact scripts associated with the tree&#8217;s outcomes. Results measures can be used as the Goal and / or constraints within Propose Best Outcomes.","The Measures table consists of:","Name - name of the measure as specified in the Add Embedded Measure dialog, the Create Measures dialog, or from a referenced measure.","Type - type of measure (e.g., Average, Max, Min), as specified when the measure is created.","Source - source of the measure, either Input, Tree Results, or Impact Results.","Label - by default, the label is the same as the name; the user can type in a different label. The label is used to identify the column used for this measure on the other editors.","Show - check this tick box to make this measure visible and available on the Assisted Design Project Tree editor; uncheck to hide the measure.","Format - determines how the measure will appear on the Assisted Design Project Tree editor. To change the format, click on the Measure format button @ to launch the Measure Presentation dialog.","To change the order of the rows in the Measures table, right click on a measure and select one of the following:","Select Move Up or Move Down to move measure up or down a row; select Move Top or Move Bottom to move the measure to the top or the bottom of the table.","To edit a measure, right click on a measure and select Open Editor. See Create an embedded measure for information on using the embedded measure editor. ","To create embedded measures from Project Characteristics, drag and drop a Project Characteristic from the Resources window into the editor. The Create Measures dialog is displayed. See Create measures using Project Characteristics for more information. ","To delete a measure from the table, right click on a measure and select Delete.","Measures Sub editor","MeasuresSubeditor.htm");
Page[5]=new Array("The Measure Presentation dialog allows the user to specify how the measure will appear on the Assisted Design Project Tree editor. ","The Measure Presentation dialog allows the user to specify the following formatting:","Prefix - specify a prefix for the result (e.g., currency abbreviation).","Precision - select the decimal precision of the result.","Scaling - specify scaling for the result (e.g., Thousands, Millions, etc.).","Scaling Suffix - specify a custom suffix to represent the scaling selected (other than the default K for Thousands, etc.).","Suffix - specify a suffix for the result (e.g., &quot;USD&quot;, &quot;Per Annum&quot;, etc.).","Measure Presentation Dialog","MeasurePresentationDialog.htm");
Page[6]=new Array("The Tree Growing Parameters sub editor allows the user to specify, from all the mapped characteristics in the logical data source, which ones to use as predictor characteristics and which to use as the target characteristic. Rounding can also be specified for predictor characteristics; this user defined rounding will be used to create the intervals in tree growing processes. The user must also select which tree growing profile to use in building the tree, and whether to include measure results in the Next Best Split analysis. ","The Tree Growing Parameters sub editor can be activated by double clicking on Tree Growing Parameters in the Navigator window. It will open in the main editor window.","By default, the characteristics metadata in the table or tree on this screen are limited to name only; by clicking on the Column Display button, the user can display other columns as well:","The Tree Growing Parameters sub editor consists of:","Tree Growing Profile  ","Select the profile that contains the settings best suited to create segmentation using Intelligent Tree Growing procedures (Grow and Next Best Split) for this Assisted Design Project.","Logical Characteristics","The logical characteristics tree/table displays all the logical data sources that are published to the node in the system composition where the Assisted Design Project has been created, and have been mapped to physical characteristics. The characteristics can be used as Target or Predictor Characteristics. Use the filters to identify the mapped logical data source characteristics to use as predictor and target characteristics.","@  Show Tree View &#8211; toggle display of logical characteristics between tree or table view. ","@ Select Characteristic &#8211; type a word or part of a word next to the Search icon and the Select Characteristic dialog will focus in on a particular characteristic or set of characteristics.","Using the * wildcard, will search all the available logical data sources to display any characteristic name and logical data source name that contains the text. Matching is not case sensitive, so you could use * to match Applicant.Scores.Risk Score with Scor*risk for example.","@  Filter for Data Types &#8211; limit the display of characteristics to one or more types (e.g. String, Integer, Decimal, etc.).","Target Characteristic &#8211; select the target characteristic from the list of tree growing characteristics by clicking on the select target button @ or by using the context pop-up menu. Click on @ to remove the target characteristic.","Predictor Characteristics ","Select the predictor characteristics from the list of mapped characteristics. Click copy @, copy all @, remove @, and remove all @ to select predictors. The pop-up menu can be used as well. ","Suppress user defined rounding - suppresses the rounding settings defined in the Predictor Characteristics window during tree growing.","Use Rounding - use the rounding defined in the Rounding column for the characteristic specified during tree growing.","Rounding - for numerics, right click on the Rounding icon to toggle between: ","@ Round to the specified number of decimal places.","@ Round to the nearest increment as specified (e.g., 100).","Measure Results","Include measure results during next best split &#8211; select the check box to include Assisted Design Project measure results in the Next Best Split analysis. ","The performance of Next Best Split will be slower if the user chooses to calculate measures, especially if many predictors are included.","Tree Growing Parameters sub editor","TreeGrowingParameterssubeditor.htm");
Page[7]=new Array("The Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor allows the user to specify all the parameters that control the behaviour of the Propose Best Outcomes analytical process. This includes defining the configuration for goal, constraints and impact scripts.","Project Characteristics are available in the Resources window to enable the user to create goal measures and constraint definition measures from within the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.","Goal Definition","Specifies the goal to be used for the Propose Best Outcomes algorithm in assisted strategy design.","Goal Name: Specify a label for the goal (e.g., Minimise Expected Bad Debt).","Goal Direction: Specify the goal direction, either Maximise or Minimise","Goal Measure: Specify the measure on which to base the goal (e.g., 'Total Expected Bad Debt'). This can be any Results measure available to the assisted design project (as generated from tree outcome execution or impact execution script) that is of the type Sum/Total or Count. The drop down menu is empty until Results measures are generated in the Measures sub editor, or a goal measure is created from a Project Characteristic.","To create a goal measure from a Project Characteristic, drag and drop a Project Characteristic from the Resources window into the Goal Measure field. The Create Measure dialog is displayed. The only measure types allowed are Count and Sum. Once the measure has been created, it will appear in the drop down menu. See Create measures using Project Characteristics for more information.","Constraints Definitions","Specifies constraints placed on the goal. ","Name: Specify a label for the constraint.","Measure: Specify the measure on which to base the constraint, from the drop down list of measures from Measures sub editor. These include measures of the type Count and Sum, and reflect only Result and Impact characteristics.  ","To create constraint measures from Project Characteristics, drag and drop one or more Project Characteristics from the Resources window into the Measure field. The Create Measures dialog is displayed. Once the measure(s) have been created, they will appear in the drop down menu. See Create measures using Project Characteristics for more information.","Direction: Specify the constraint direction, either At least or At most.","Outcome Impact Scripts","Impact scripts can be assigned by dragging and dropping from Derived Data Script in the Resources window (by reference), or by right clicking and selecting Add Embedded Impact Script. By reference impact scripts can use any defined data script component available in the Resources window, including those using user functions.","An impact script can be opened from within the Outcome Impact Scripts table by right clicking on the script and selecting Open Editor. The script will open in the scripting editor.","See Outcome Impact Scripts for details.","Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor","OutcomeAssignmentParameterssubeditor.htm");
Page[8]=new Array("Impact scripts provide a mechanism for the user to measure the expected impact of assigning a particular outcome to one or more segments and thereby evaluate the effectiveness of the strategy. The user can configure impact scripts, which are &quot;executed&quot; immediately following tree execution to create Impact Characteristics, which in turn form the basis of Impact Measures used both in Review and Analysis and to drive Propose Best Outcomes. An impact script can be defined for each outcome defined in the Assisted Design Project. For example, if the tree is assigning an interest rate, an impact script might be used to calculate the expected take-up for different groups of customers offered particular rates. This would all be specified within the impact script. The same impact script may be used for several or all outcomes in the tree scenario. Impact scripts are essentially derived data scripts and can be embedded or referenced within the Assisted Design Project.","The use of Impact Scripts is optional; however, if an impact script is defined for one outcome, an impact script must be defined for all, to ensure impact measures are calculated for all outcomes.","Impact scripts can be assigned by dragging and dropping from Derived Data Script in the Resources window (by reference), or by right clicking and selecting Add Embedded Impact Script. By reference impact scripts can use any defined data script component available in the Resources window, including those using user functions. ","The user can also drag and drop Project Characteristics into the scripting editor. This is particularly relevant when the user wants to create an impact script based on tree results (e.g., a decision or a result characteristic such as Interest Rate or Credit Limit). The results generated by scorecard trees and decision setter trees, for example, are found under Tree Results in the Resources window. They are not written out as logical data set characteristics (found under Results LDS); instead, these trees write out implicit results such as the Decision Category or Decision Text (found under Typical LDM in the Tree Results). The user can drag and drop these types of tree results into the editor, as in the following line of script:","If 'Test_Results.New Tree.Decision Category' = &quot;Accept&quot; THEN","In this example the script is picking up the test result, not an actual logical data set characteristic. ","Outcome Impact Scripts","OutcomeImpactScripts.htm");
Page[9]=new Array("An empty Assisted Design Project component can be created from the Explorer window. ","You will only be able to add a component type that has been allowed for the selected folder. If it is not allowed it will not be listed and therefore cannot be selected. ","To create an empty Assisted Design Project  ","From the Explorer window right click at the required folder level and select Add component.","Select Assisted Design Project.","In the Create component dialog enter the name of the Assisted Design Project.","If you wish to have the new component display in the Main editor on creation, ensure that the Open new components in component editor tick box is selected.","Click on the OK button.","The Assisted Design Project Tree editor will open allowing you to build the Assisted Design Project.<br>The Assisted Design Project component name will be listed in the Explorer window under the selected folder level. The component is identified by the @ icon.","If you do not wish the editor to open, de-select the tick box. From the Explorer window select the Assisted Design Project and select Open. ","Until it has been promoted for use in the public domain, the tree created within the Assisted Design Project can not be used by other trees or components.","Create an Assisted Design Project from the Explorer","CreateanAssistedDesignProjectfromtheExplorer.htm");
Page[10]=new Array("A new Assisted Design Project component can be created from an existing tree in the Explorer window. ","To create an Assisted Design Project from an existing tree","From the Explorer window right click on an existing tree and select New Assisted Design Project.","In the Create component dialog enter the name of the Assisted Design Project.","Select the Open new components in component editor check box.","Click on the OK button.<br>The  Assisted Design Project Tree editor will open allowing you to  build the Assisted Design Project. <br>The Assisted Design Project component name will be listed in the Explorer under the selected folder level. ","If you do not wish the editor to open, keep the check box de-selected.  From the Explorer window right click on the Assisted Design Project component and select Open. The structure of the tree in the Assisted Design Project will be identical to that of the original tree. The user will still need to select an analysis data set, map the characteristics, and specify the tree growing parameters.","Once the tree has been modified and saved, you can use it to replace the original tree or merge it with the original tree.","Until it has been promoted for use in the public domain, the tree created within the Assisted Design Project can not be used by other trees or components.","Create an Assisted Design Project from an existing tree","CreateanAssistedDesignProjectfromanexistingtree.htm");
Page[11]=new Array("A new Assisted Design Project component can be created from a tree that exists as part of a process flow. ","To create an Assisted Design Project from a tree in a decision process flow","From the Decision Process Flow editor right click on a tree node and select Create Assisted Design Project. If the node selected does not contain a tree, the option will be greyed out.","In the Create Component dialog enter the name of the Assisted Design Project and click on the OK button.","The Assisted Design Project component name will be listed in the Explorer window under the folder containing the original tree. ","You should now right click on the Assisted Design Project component and select Open. The Assisted Design Project Tree editor will open allowing you to build the Assisted Design Project.  ","The structure of the tree in the Assisted Design Project will be identical to that of the original tree. The user will still need to select an analysis data set, map the characteristics, and specify the tree growing parameters.","Once the tree has been modified and saved, you can use it to replace the original tree, or merge it with the original tree, or promote it as a challenger to the original tree within the originator decision process flow.","Until it has been promoted for use in the public domain, the tree created within the Assisted Design Project can not be used by other trees or components.","Create an Assisted Design Project from a tree in a decision process flow","CreateanAssistedDesignProjectfromatreeinadecisionprocessflow.htm");
Page[12]=new Array("It is possible to create and maintain Assisted Strategy Design projects based on multi-process trees (known as Decision Process Trees). This means that Business Objective flows and trees can be imported from SMG3 into Strategy Manager, and Assisted Strategy Design can be used to validate the accuracy of the conversion by calculation of key measures against trees. Assisted Strategy Design projects based on multi-process trees behave consistently with Assisted Strategy Design projects based on any other types of trees.","See the SMG3 File Import help for more information about the conversion of Business Objective Flows to Decision Process Flows.","Support for multi-process trees within Assisted Strategy Design","Supportformulti-processtreeswithinAssistedStrategyDesign.htm");
Page[13]=new Array("The user can import a strategy tree into the Assisted Design Project to replace the existing tree scenario. This is useful, for example, when the user has created an Assisted Design Project from a template, and then imports a specific existing tree from the solution to make it the subject of the Assisted Design Project. This is also useful when the user wants to refresh an existing Assisted Design Project with a new copy of the original tree, as in cases where the operational tree has been modified since the Assisted Design Project was created.","To import a strategy tree to replace the existing tree scenario","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, click on the &quot;Import an existing tree&quot; button @. ","The Please select a tree dialog is displayed:","Select an available tree and click on the OK button.","The tree segmentation in the Assisted Design Project is replaced by the imported tree segmentation. Outcomes of the imported strategy tree are merged with those outcomes from the existing Assisted Design Project tree scenario. ","If the outcome assignments from the Assisted Design Project tree scenario are incompatible with those of the imported tree (e.g., different Treatment Table Design or different component type), then only the outcome names are used and the assigned components are not used.","Duplicate outcomes are removed to preserve a single copy of each outcome (i.e., if the outcome name, outcome assignment type and assigned component name are the same).","Import a strategy tree to replace the existing tree scenario","Importastrategytreetoreplacetheexistingtreescenario.htm");
Page[14]=new Array("The Assisted Design Project is built from the Assisted Design Project component displayed in the Explorer window.","To build an Assisted Design Project","From the Explorer window right click on the required Assisted Design Project component and select Open.","If the Assisted Design Project component has not previously been opened (i.e., you are working with a newly created, empty tree), you are prompted to select the type of tree with which to work:","All tree types allowed in the current folder will be listed.","Select the type of tree, and click on the Apply button.","A tree scenario is created within the Tree sub editor. If this is a new project, the tree is empty, if the project has been created from an existing tree, the tree structure is identical to that of the original tree. ","The content of the editor depends on the type of tree you have selected to work with. In addition, the Navigator window displays elements unique to the Assisted Design Project:","From the Navigator window, right click on Analysis Data Set and select Open Editor to display the Analysis Data Set sub editor.","Specify an embedded or by reference Input Data Connection to work with as the primary data set, and an optional comparison data set. Filters can also be specified for each.","From the Navigator window, right click on Measures and select Open Editor to display the Measures sub editor. ","Reference or embed any measures you want to use to evaluate the strategy.","Project Characteristics enable you to create measures at the point they are required during the Assisted Design process - from within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, the Measures sub editor, or the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.","From the Navigator window, right click on Tree Growing Parameters and select Open Editor to display the  Tree Growing Parameters sub editor.  ","Select the  target and predictor characteristics, then specify the  tree growing profile.","From the Navigator window, right click on Outcome Assignment Parameters and select Open Editor to display the  Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.  ","Specify the Goal, Constraints and Impacts Scripts that control the behaviour of the Propose Best Outcomes analytical process.","From the Navigator window, right click on New Tree and select Open Editor to display the  Tree sub editor.","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, you can grow the tree from any leaf node or perform next best split analysis, and you can can set outcome assignment goals and use Propose Best Outcomes to automatically select outcomes that best satisfy your business goals and constraints.","Build an Assisted Design Project","BuildanAssistedDesignProject.htm");
Page[15]=new Array("The Analysis Data Set is an embedded or by reference Input Data Connection. The user must specify a primary analysis data set, and an optional comparison data set. The user can add an embedded or by reference filter to either data set to limit the number of records that will be included in the analysis","To specify an embedded Input Data Connection","With the Analysis Data Set sub editor open.","Where no Input Data Connection has been set, you can right click on the Primary Data Set field, and select Insert Embedded: ","Alternatively, you can drag Embedded Input Data Connection from the Palette:","and drop it into the Primary Data Set field:","The Insert Embedded Component dialog is displayed:","Rename the component and click on the OK button to open the Input Data Connection editor.","Once the embedded Input Data Connection has been created, you can right click on the Input data connection field to open the Input Data Connection editor, clear the field, or rename the component:","Repeat steps 2 to 7 to insert an embedded Input Data Connection into the Comparison data set field, if desired. ","(Optional) If desired, you can add an embedded or by reference filter to limit the number of records that will be included in the analysis, for either the primary data set or the comparison data set, or both.","To add an existing filter to either data set, expand the Filter list in the Resources window to display the available filters. For example:"," @","Drag and drop the required filter into the Primary filter or Comparison filter field.","To create a new filter, right click in the filter field and select Insert Embedded to display the Insert Embedded Component dialog:","Rename the filter to something meaningful, and click on the OK button to display the filter scripting editor. This editor is a standard scripting editor and is very similar to the Boolean Expression editor.","Create the filter script, click on the Compile Script button to validate it, then click on the close button to exit the scripting editor.","The Analysis Data Set is now configured for use.","The Input Data Connection editor includes a feature that is unique to embedded Input Data Connections for Assisted Design Projects.","The list of filters in the Logical Characteristics window includes the ability to limit the display of unmapped characteristics to one or more of the following types: Data Set Filter lists unmapped characteristics required for the specified data set filter; Segmentation filter for all tree segmentation characteristics; Measures filter for all measure input characteristics, including filters; Tree Growing filter for all segmentation input characteristics and characteristics referenced in tree growing parameters; Tree Testing filter for all segmentation and assignment input characteristics; and Impact Analysis filter for all characteristics used in impact scripts.","This list of filters is not included for the by reference Input Data Connections.","Specify an embedded Input Data Connection","SpecifyanembeddedInputDataConnection.htm");
Page[16]=new Array("The Analysis Data Set is an embedded or by reference Input Data Connection. The user must specify a primary analysis data set, and an optional comparison data set. The user can add an embedded or by reference filter to either data set to limit the number of records that will be included in the analysis","To specify a by reference Input Data Connection","With the Analysis Data Set sub editor open","Drag the Input Data Connection component from the Resources window:","and drop it into the Input data connection field:","Dropping the component into the field automatically triggers the validation functionality, as in the following example where the source is invalid:","Once the Input Data Connection has been specified, you can right click on the Input data connection field to open the Input Data Connection editor, clear the field, or insert an embedded component in its place:","Repeat steps 2 and 3 to specify a by reference Input Data Connection into the Comparison data set field, if desired. ","(Optional) If desired, you can add an embedded or by reference filter to limit the number of records that will be included in the analysis, for either the primary data set or the comparison data set, or both.","To add an existing filter to either data set, expand the Filter list in the Resources window to display the available filters. For example:"," @","Drag and drop the required filter into the Primary filter or Comparison filter field.","To create a new filter, right click in the filter field and select Insert Embedded to display the Insert Embedded Component dialog:","Rename the filter to something meaningful, and click on the OK button to display the filter scripting editor. This editor is a standard scripting editor and is very similar to the Boolean Expression editor.","Create the filter script, click on the Compile Script button to validate it, then click on the close button to exit the scripting editor.","The Analysis Data Set is now configured for use.","Specify a by reference Input Data Connection","SpecifyabyreferenceInputDataConnection.htm");
Page[17]=new Array("Measures can be referenced from within the Assisted Design Project; this means they can be changed from outside the project and may be shared by other projects. Alternatively, a measure can be created from within the Measure sub editor and will be embedded into the Assisted Design Project. This means the measure is only used by this project and can only be edited from within this project. ","To add a referenced measure to an Assisted Design Project","With the Measures sub editor open, click on the Resources window. The valid component types will be listed.","Expand the Measure list to display all the available measures. For example:","Drag and drop the required measure into the Measures table.","The measure can now be used in the Assisted Design Project. ","Add a referenced measure","Addareferencedmeasure.htm");
Page[18]=new Array("Measures can be referenced from within the Assisted Design Project. This means they can be changed from outside the project and may be shared by other projects. Alternatively, a measure can be created from within the Measure sub editor and will be embedded into the Assisted Design Project. This means the measure is only used by this project and can only be edited from within this project.","You can also create embedded measures using Project Characteristics at the point they are required during the Assisted Design process from within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, the Measures sub editor, or the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor. See Create measures using Project Characteristics for details.","To create an embedded measure in an Assisted Design Project","With the Measures sub editor open, right click in the Measures table and select Add Embedded Measure. The Insert Embedded Component dialog is displayed:","Overtype Embedded Measure with a unique name (within the project) for the measure, and click on the OK button. The embedded measure editor is displayed:","The fields in this editor are dependent on the measure type chosen.","From the Measure type drop-down list, select the type of measure to create (choices are: Average, Count, Max, Min, Ratio, Std Deviation, Sum).","Click on the browse @ button to select the characteristic to use as the basis for the measure. ","In Assisted Design the characteristic field is optional for COUNT measures (it will always count the records in the analysis data set).","(Optional) If desired, you can add an existing filter to the measure to limit the range of values that are used to create the measure, or create a new filter; the default is &lt;All Cases&gt;. ","To add an existing filter to the measure, expand the Filter list in the Resources window to display all the available filters. For example:","Drag and drop the required filter into the &lt;All Cases&gt; field.","To create a new filter, right click in the &lt;All Cases&gt; field and select Insert Embedded to display the Insert Embedded Component dialog:","Rename the filter to something meaningful, and click on the OK button to display the filter scripting editor. This editor is a standard scripting editor and is very similar to the Boolean Expression editor.","Create the filter script, click on the Compile Script button @ to validate it, then click on the close button @ to exit the scripting editor.","The embedded measure is now complete:  ","Click on the close button @ to exit the Embedded Measure editor and return to the Measures sub editor.","The embedded measure is listed at the end of the Measures table, and is ready to be used in the Assisted Design Project.","Create an embedded measure","Createanembeddedmeasure.htm");
Page[19]=new Array("Project Characteristics make it simple for the user to create measures at the point they are required during the Assisted Design process - from within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, the Measures sub editor, or the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.","Project Characteristics are grouped together in the Resources window into folders that reflect how the characteristics are currently used within the Assisted Design Project. The order of the folders reflects the user&#8217;s typical workflow order in Assisted Strategy Design; from Review and Analysis, through to Intelligent Tree Growing, and onto impact analysis and Propose Best Outcomes.","For example, if the user is growing a tree in the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, they can drag a set of Project Characteristics into the editor and turn them into measures. The user can then calculate these measures in the tree scenario, and decide on the basis of review and analysis whether to use these characteristics as predictors or not.","Similarly, if the user is working in the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor, they may want to select a constraint measure that has not yet been defined. Without leaving the editor, the user can drag and drop a characteristic from the Tree Results or Impact Results into the Constraints Definitions Measure field and the Create Measures dialog will open. The user can then create a measure which will be immediately added to the drop down list of available constraint measures.","Measures can be created from one characteristic at a time; or (within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor and the Measures sub editor) measures can be created from multiple characteristics of the same type. See Create measures using Project Characteristics for details.","Project Characteristics are grouped together in the Resources window by type:","Allows the user to access characteristics that have been mapped, irrespective of whether they are used in the project.","For Decision Process Trees, the Project Characteristics provide the user with access to the input and results characteristics for all of the assigned processes; these can be used to create new measures.","If the sequence includes one or more Scorecard or Decision Setter processes, additional nodes will appear in the Tree Results for each process. These include the typical results characteristics for that process type. For Decision Process Trees they retrieve mapping information for the processes from the Decision Process Sequence, so this information is used inside the Assisted Strategy Design project. This means that if characteristics are mapped then they will appear as mapped results characteristics, and will be removed from the generic results data model section. ","Displaying full characteristic names","There are two ways to display the full name of a characteristic:","Position the cursor over the characteristic name to display a tool tip.","Right click on Project Characteristics, and select Show Path from the context menu.","The full name will now appear in the Resources window after the characteristic name, in parentheses:","Using Project Characteristics to create measures: an overview","anoverview.htm");
Page[20]=new Array("Project Characteristics are available in the Resources window to enable the user to create embedded measures at the point they are required during the Assisted Design process - from within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, the Measures sub editor, or the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.","Measures can be created from one characteristic at a time; or (within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor and the Measures sub editor) measures can be created from multiple characteristics of the same type.","To create measures using a single Project Characteristic","With the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, the Measures sub editor, or the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor open, drag and drop a characteristic from the list of Project Characteristics in the Resources window into the appropriate spot in the editor.","For the Assisted Design Project Tree editor and the Measures sub editor, you can drop characteristics anywhere in the editor. For the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor, you can only drop characteristics into the Goal Measure field or a Constraint Definitions Measure field. See the different editors for details.","The Create Measures dialog is displayed, with the name of the selected characteristic displayed.","Select at least one measure type for the selected characteristic; the measure type for string characteristics is limited to Count. For each measure type selected, a different embedded measure will be created.","The full characteristic name is displayed in the Suffix field; you can overwrite this default name with a shorter, more manageable name that will be used when the measure is displayed in the various editors.","For Count measures only, you can enter values in the Value area of the dialog. Each value added will create a different embedded measure, with the value incorporated as a filter. Right click in the Value area to add a new row and enter a value: Note: The value(s) you enter should match the discrete values expected for the characteristic. E.g., G and P are used here for the characteristic &quot;Card Type&quot;:","Click OK to save and close the dialog.","The measure or measures are displayed in the appropriate place within the editor.","To create measures using multiple Project Characteristics","With the Assisted Design Project Tree editor or the Measures sub editor open, select multiple characteristics by clicking on the required Project Characteristics in the Resources window whilst holding down the CTRL key on the keyboard. Note that the characteristics must be of the same type.","Drag and drop the characteristics anywhere in the editor.","The Create Measures dialog is displayed, with the names of the selected characteristics displayed.","Select at least one measure type for the selected characteristics; the measure type for string characteristics is limited to Count. For each measure type selected, a different embedded measure will be created.","The Suffix field is disabled when multiple Project Characteristics have been selected.","For Count measures only, you can enter values in the Value area of the dialog. Each value added will create a different embedded measure, with the value incorporated as a filter. Right click in the Value area to add a new row and enter a value:","Click OK to save and close the dialog.","The measures are displayed in the appropriate place within the editor.","Create measures using Project Characteristics","CreatemeasuresusingProjectCharacteristics.htm");
Page[21]=new Array("Embedded measures can be created from Project Characteristics from within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, the Measures sub editor, or the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor. Measures can be created from one characteristic at a time; or (within the Assisted Design Project Tree editor and the Measures sub editor) measures can be created from multiple characteristics of the same type.","Project Characteristics - Displays the name(s) of the selected Project Characteristic(s).","Measure Types - Select at least one measure type for the selected characteristic(s); the measure type for string characteristics is limited to Count. For each measure type selected, a different embedded measure will be created. ","Suffix - If a single Project Characteristic has been selected, the full characteristic name is displayed in the Suffix field; the user can overwrite this default name with a shorter, more manageable name that will be used when the measure is displayed in the various editors. If multiple Project Characteristics have been selected, this field is disabled.","Value - For Count measures only, the user can enter values in the Value area of the dialog. Each value added will create a different embedded measure, with the value incorporated as a filter. Right click in the Value area to add a new row and enter a value:","Create Measures dialog","CreateMeasuresdialog.htm");
Page[22]=new Array("An embedded goal measure can be created from Project Characteristics from within the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.","Project Characteristic - Displays the name of the selected Project Characteristic.","Measure Types - Select at least one measure type for the selected characteristic; the measure type for string characteristics is limited to Count. ","Suffix - The full characteristic name is displayed in the Suffix field; the user can overwrite this default name with a shorter, more manageable name that will be used when the measure is displayed in the various editors.","Add value - For Count measures only, the user can enter a value in the Add value field of the dialog:","Create Goal Measures dialog","CreateGoalMeasuresdialog.htm");
Page[23]=new Array("From the Tree Growing Parameters sub editor, the user can specify, from all the characteristics in the logical data source, which ones to use as predictor characteristics and which to use as the target characteristic. Rounding can also be specified for predictor characteristics; this user defined rounding will be used to create the intervals in tree growing processes. If not specified, the user can still work with the Assisted Design Project, but can&#8217;t perform next best split analysis or tree growing.","To select tree growing parameters","In the Tree Growing Parameters sub editor, using the filters if necessary, identify and select the target characteristic from the list of tree growing characteristics, then click on the Add Target button @ to add the characteristic to the Target Characteristic field. Only mapped characteristics will be shown.","To clear the Target Characteristic field, click on the Clear Target button @.","Select the predictor characteristics from the list, and click on the Add Selected Predictor(s) button @ to add them to the Predictor Characteristics window. Click on the Add All Predictors button @ to add all the characteristics at once.","To remove characteristics from the list of predictor characteristics, click on the Remove Selected Predictor(s) button @ or on the Remove All Predictors button @ to remove all characteristics.","For numeric characteristics, check the Rounding tick box to select rounding. ","Right click the rounding icon to choose between: @ Round to the specified number of decimal places, and @ Round to the nearest increment as specified (e.g., 100).","On closing the editor, click on the Save button in the Question dialog to save the parameter assignments.","You should now select a tree growing profile.","Select tree growing parameters","Selecttreegrowingparameters.htm");
Page[24]=new Array("From the Tree Growing Parameters sub editor, the user must select a tree growing profile for either CHAID or CART analysis from the drop down menu.","CHAID (Chi-square Automatic Interaction Detector) is a tree-based exploratory method used to study the relationship between a target characteristic and a series of predictors. The purpose of CHAID is to produce a meaningful set of rules for segmentation of a population.","While the CHAID algorithm allows splits with any number of child nodes, limited only by the constraints imposed by the profile selected, CART (i.e., C&amp;RT, Classification And Regression Tree) permits only binary splits.","The tree growing profile contains the various settings used to create segmentation that best conforms to the user's requirements.","The Boolean splits profile uses CART analysis (binary splits only).","The other profiles use CHAID analysis, and allow splits of from up to 3 intervals to up to 10 intervals. Splits of up to 3 intervals is the default.","Tree Growing Profile overview","TreeGrowingProfileoverview.htm");
Page[25]=new Array("The user can allow Assisted Design to grow the tree one or more levels automatically, by allowing the software to choose which characteristics are most predictive, and how best to split them. The branch will grow according to the characteristics and tree growing profile specified, without any initial analysis.","For tree growing to work, both the client and the server must have access to the data source. Read only access is enough.","The resulting branch displays the automatically identified sub segments that will provide the best rules based solution to the business problem.","A branch can only be grown from a leaf node of a tree.","Measures can be calculated for new leaf nodes, both before and after making assignments, for both input measures and results measures.","To grow a branch of the tree","From the  Assisted Design Project Tree editor, right click on a leaf node of the tree and select Grow.","The branch grows according to the  characteristics and  tree growing profile specified.","Provide leaf node names, outcome assignments, etc., according to the type of tree selected for the Assisted Design Project.","You can continue to prune and grow leaf nodes of the branch until you are satisfied with the results.","On closing the editor, click on the Save button in the Question dialog to save the Assisted Design Project.","You can test the Assisted Design scenario, either manually or as part of a batch test.","You can compare the measure results for the primary data set and a comparison data set to validate the scenario.","You can reopen and modify the Assisted Design Project whenever you want, however, the tree you have created can not be used by other components until it has been promoted for use as a new tree or it has been used to replace or merge with an original tree.","Grow a branch of a tree","Growabranchofatree.htm");
Page[26]=new Array("The user can use Next Best Split to identify and rank a set of predictors for the next level of the tree. The user will assess the proposed splits and choose which one to apply to the tree, based on statistical measures and measures from the Assisted Design Project.","Next Best Split analysis can only be performed for a leaf node of a tree.","To include Assisted Design Project measures in the Next Best Split analysis, you must previously have selected the &quot;Include measure results during next best split&quot; check box on the Tree Growing Parameters sub editor. The performance of Next Best Split will be slower if you choose to calculate measures, especially if many predictors are included.","To perform Next Best Split analysis","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, right click on a leaf node of the tree and select Next Best Split.","The software identifies and ranks a set of predictors for the next level of the tree in the Next Best Split dialog, but does not change the structure of the strategy tree.","Click on the column button @ to choose from the available measures to assess the strengths of the various predictors. Statistical measures are available by default; you can also choose to display measures from the Assisted Design Project. ","Expand each predictor to review the measures for each interval.","Select the predictor that best meets your objectives, and click on the Apply @ button to apply the selected split to the tree.","The add split dialog will be activated allowing you to preserve existing assignments at the leaf node. Click on the Confirm button.","Provide leaf node names, outcome assignments, etc., according to the type of tree selected for the Assisted Design Project. ","You can recalculate existing measures for new leaf nodes, to help assess which splits best meet user objectives, or add new measures.","You can continue to prune and grow leaf nodes off the branch until you are satisfied with the results.","On closing the editor, click on the Save button in the Question dialog to save the Assisted Design Project.","You can test the Assisted Design scenario, either manually or as part of a batch test within the Assisted Design Project.","You can compare the measure results for the primary data set and a comparison data set to validate the scenario.","You can reopen and modify the Assisted Design Project whenever you want; however, the tree you have created can not be used by other components until it has been promoted for use as a new tree or it has been used to replace or merge with an original tree.","Perform Next Best Split analysis","PerformNextBestSplitanalysis.htm");
Page[27]=new Array("Next Best Split analysis automatically identifies and ranks a set of splits for the next level of the tree based on the specified predictors and target characteristic, but will not change the structure of the strategy tree until a split is applied to the tree. ","The Next Best Split dialog identifies the splits and measures their strengths relative to one another. It shows the splits suggested by the automatic tree growing procedure in the lower window. ","To include Assisted Design Project measures in the Next Best Split analysis, the user must previously have selected the &quot;Include measure results during next best split&quot; check box on the Tree Growing Parameters sub editor. Input measures from the project will be calculated and available for display. Input measures based on the Target characteristic, e.g. #Goods, are automatically selected for display.","Click on the column display button @ to expand the list of available measures. Selected measures will appear in the Analysis table. Assisted Design Project measures will only appear in the menu if the user has previously selected the &quot;Include measure results during next best split&quot; check box on the Tree Growing Parameters sub editor:","To apply a selected split to the tree the user can click on the Apply button.","The selected split will create the next layer of the strategy tree for the selected branch, as an embedded split. Growing a branch in this way creates one level of a branch at a time. When a new split is added to a leaf node, either manually or via Next Best Split, and that leaf node already had an assignment to an outcome configured, the tree editor displays the Add Split repair dialog at the bottom of the editor: ","This dialog allows the user to preserve the outcome assignment that was in place prior to using Next Best Split, and propagate it to all or selected intervals on the new split.","Next Best Split dialog","NextBestSplitdialog.htm");
Page[28]=new Array("The user can use the Measures sub editor to define measures based on any characteristics in their Analysis Data Set, and use these measures to assess the current strategy tree as well as to direct strategy design/tree growing. Calculate measures will calculate all measures defined in the Assisted Design Project, including input measures and results, as well as impact measures. Informed by measures, the user can select a node within the tree to work on, for example; a low risk score band exhibiting a higher than expected delinquency rate, with significant population size.","In addition, the user can compare measures between the Primary Analysis Data Set and the Comparison data set (as identified in the Analysis Data Set sub editor).","All mappings must be provided before Calculate can proceed (warnings are displayed to the user). All tree assignments must be valid before calculating results based measures.","Measure results will be cleared when they become out of date as a result of changes in the Analysis Data Set, Mappings, Measures or Tree.","To calculate measures against a tree scenario","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, click on the Calculate measures button @. ","The measures are calculated for each node of the tree, as in the following example:","Measures are also calculated by outcome. Click the Show/Hide Measures button @ to display the calculated measures in the Outcomes table.","You can modify the tree and create new measures as desired, then recalculate the measures until you are satisfied with the segmentation results.","Click on the column display button @ to limit the display of measures in the Tree sub editor. Any changes to the display of measures in the Tree table will be reflected in the Outcomes table; so columns that are hidden or reordered in the Table column will be hidden or reordered automatically in the Outcomes table as well.","Calculate measures against a tree scenario","Calculatemeasuresagainstatreescenario.htm");
Page[29]=new Array("The user can validate a scenario by using a comparison data set, which might be a subset of the primary data set or a validation sample taken from a different observation period. The Compare Measures mode will allow the user to directly compare the measure results from both data sets, side by side.","To compare measures between primary and comparison data sets","Define a primary and comparison data set (and optional filter) as described in the Analysis Data Set sub editor.","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, click on the Enter measure comparison mode button @.","The measures are calculated for each node of the tree, and displayed for both the Primary Analysis Data Set and the Comparison data set, as in the following example:","The tool tip for the measures columns displays the name of the data set and the filter used, depending on which cell you are on. ","Compare Measures also displays measure for both data sets in the Outcomes table:","You can save a record of the calculated measures using the Lite Print option whilst in the Compare Measures mode. ","To exit the measure comparison mode, click the Close Comparison button.","When in measure comparison mode, all other tree operations are disabled; you can change between tree view and table view, add notes/annotations, and move the columns around, but you can not otherwise interact with the tree.","Compare measures between primary and comparison data sets","Comparemeasuresbetweenprimaryandcomparisondatasets.htm");
Page[30]=new Array("The user can use Propose Best Outcomes to propose the best outcomes to assign to leaf nodes to best satisfy the user's business goals and constraints. ","Prior to using this functionality, the user must specify the parameters that control the behaviour of the Propose Best Outcomes analytical process in the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor. This includes defining the configuration for impact scripts, goals, and constraints.","To propose best outcomes for a node, branch, or tree scenario","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, right click on the top node of the branch or tree for which you want the software to propose outcomes.","Select Assign Outcomes to display the Propose Best Outcomes Settings dialog.","Specify the constraint values for the outcome assignment goal for the selected branch.","Specify whether the assignments are for all nodes, or only for unassigned nodes.","Click OK to run the analytical process. When complete, the Propose Best Outcomes  Analysis is displayed:","If the results of the analysis are acceptable, click Apply to apply the outcomes to the original tree.","If not, you can modify the settings, i.e. the constraints and eligibility, and re-run the analysis.","Once you have applied the outcomes to the original tree, you can still click the Undo button @ to revert the tree to its original outcome assignments.","Propose Best Outcomes","ProposeBestOutcomes.htm");
Page[31]=new Array("The Propose Best Outcomes Settings dialog allows the user to specify constraint values and eligibility for the outcome assignment goal for a set of nodes.","For each outcome defined for the tree, the user can specify constraint values and eligibility. They can also specify overall constraint values. The constraints are specified in the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor.","Outcome Assignment Goal","The following information is specified in the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor and cannot be changed here by the user:","Name: Name of the goal.","Measure: Measure selected for the goal.","Direction: Goal direction (Maximum or Minimum).","Constraints","Outcome is active in scope: Shows an outcome as Active if it is currently Active (i.e., assigned to any leaf node) in the selected scope of analysis (the sub-tree/node).","Eligible: Specify the eligibility of the constraints for each outcome.","Outcome name: All outcomes defined for the tree are listed by name.","Constraint values: Specify the constraint values for each constraint. Values can be assigned overall and per outcome.","Assignments","Specify that assignments are to be made to All nodes in scope or Only unassigned nodes in scope.","In the above example, the goal of the analysis is to maximise interest income for the selected branch. The results of the analysis must include at least 2,000 accepts for applicants at 6.9% and 7.9%; and the total bad debt allowed for the selected branch is 15M.","Propose Best Outcomes Settings dialog","ProposeBestOutcomesSettingsdialog.htm");
Page[32]=new Array("The Propose Best Outcomes Analysis is displayed as the result of using the Propose Best Outcomes analytical process to automatically select outcomes that best satisfy the user's business goals and constraints (as specified in the Outcome Assignment Parameters sub editor).","The name of the root node of the branch selected for the analysis is displayed at the top of the dialog, along with the goal specified for the analysis.","The results expected if the proposed assignment were to be applied to the scope of analysis is displayed in the upper window of the dialog. Move the cursor over any measure in the upper window for a tool tip that displays the original value, and the most recent value for that measure prior to running Propose Best Outcomes:","Move the cursor over the target for more information about the target value:","The eligible outcomes selected for this analysis are displayed in the lower window of the dialog.","Click Apply to apply the results of the analysis to the original tree. Click the Modify settings button to invoke the Propose Best Outcomes Settings dialog and re-run the analysis with different constraints.","Propose Best Outcomes Analysis dialog","ProposeBestOutcomesAnalysisdialog.htm");
Page[33]=new Array("Once the user has successfully created an Assisted Design Project, the user will be able to test the tree scenario using the Batch or Interactive Tester. ","The user can test an Assisted Design scenario either by manually typing in data for the input characteristics referenced in the scenario or by performing a test against the Analysis Data Set.","To perform an interactive test","To test an Assisted Design scenario manually by entering data into the relevant fields for the input characteristics, use the Interactive Tester.","To perform a batch test","To perform a batch test on an Assisted Design scenario, click on the Perform Batch Test button @. The primary analysis data set--i.e., the selected Input Data Connection and any defined filter--and all defined mappings are used. The test results are displayed under the Batch Test Results tab in the Output window. To display the batch test results at any time, click on the Show Batch Test Results button @.","Test an Assisted Design tree scenario","TestanAssistedDesigntreescenario.htm");
Page[34]=new Array("Once the Assisted Design scenario is complete, it can be saved as a new tree for use in an operational environment.","To save an Assisted Design scenario as a new tree","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, click on the Save this scenario as new tree button @.","In the Warning dialog, click on the OK button to create the tree.","In the Create component dialog enter the name of the new tree.","Select the Open new components in component editor check box.","Click on the OK button.","The new tree will open, but will also be listed in the Explorer under the same folder as the Assisted Design Project.","The new tree can now be used within other components.","Save Assisted Design scenario as a new tree","SaveAssistedDesignscenarioasanewtree.htm");
Page[35]=new Array("If an Assisted Design Project has been  created from an existing tree, the user has the option of using it to replace the original tree once it is complete.","To replace the original tree with an Assisted Design scenario","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, click on the &quot;Replace original tree with this scenario&quot; button @.","In the Warning dialog, click on the OK button to replace the original tree.","Click on the OK button again to acknowledge that the tree has been replaced.","The original tree has been replaced with the contents of the Assisted Design scenario.","Use Assisted Design scenario to replace the original tree","UseAssistedDesignscenariotoreplacetheoriginaltree.htm");
Page[36]=new Array("If an Assisted Design Project has been  created from an existing tree, the user has the option of merging it with the original tree using a test group set split. Also referred to as champion - challenger.","For the merge functionality to work, a Test Group Set must previously have been created, with the same number of test groups as there are trees being merged (ie, 2).","To merge the Assisted Design scenario with the original tree","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, click on the &quot;Merge with Original Tree&quot; button @. ","The Merge with Original Tree dialog is displayed:","Select the appropriate test group set and click on the OK button.","In the Warning dialog, click on the OK button again to acknowledge that the tree has been merged.","The original tree has been merged with the contents of the Assisted Design scenario, as in the following example:","Merge the Assisted Design scenario with the original tree","MergetheAssistedDesignscenariowiththeoriginaltree.htm");
Page[37]=new Array("If an Assisted Design Project has been created from a tree that exists as part of a process flow, the user has the option of promoting it for use within the original decision process flow as challenger to the original tree.","For the promotion to work, a Test Group Set must previously have been created, with the same number of test groups as there are trees being merged, ie, 2.","To promote the Assisted Design scenario as challenger within the original decision process flow","From the Assisted Design Project Tree editor, click on the &quot;Promote as Challenger within the original Decision Process Flow&quot; button @. ","The Merge with Original Tree dialog is displayed:","Select the appropriate test group set and click on the OK button. The Create component dialog is displayed:","Provide a name for the new challenger tree, and click OK to create the tree.","The new tree is listed in the Explorer and is added to the original decision process flow as challenger to the original tree from which it was created:","Promote the Assisted Design scenario as challenger within the original decision process flow","PromotetheAssistedDesignscenarioaschallengerwithintheoriginaldecisionprocessflow.htm");
Page[38]=new Array("Lite Print has been implemented in Assisted Design, enabling the user to print the tree scenario with measure results (see the Printing overview for more information on the Lite Print option).","To print the tree scenario with measure results","From the Main menu bar, click on Tools.","Select Options to display the Options dialog.","Click on the Printing icon, and select the lite radio button:","Click OK to close the dialog.","Now, with the Assisted Design Project displayed in the main editor, select Print from the File menu.","Select Printable Lit Doc to produce a print of the tree scenario.","From here you can obtain a snapshot print of the Assisted Design Project displayed in the main editor. It will print out the tree along with any measures that have been calculated against the segmentation or against the outcomes. ","You can print from the Compare Measures mode to get a print of the measure results for both data sets.","Print the tree scenario with measure results","Printthetreescenariowithmeasureresults.htm");
var PageCount=39;

function search(SearchWord){
var Result="";
var NrRes=0;
Result='<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01 Transitional//EN" "http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/loose.dtd">\n';
Result+="<html>\n";
Result+="<head>\n";
Result+="<title>Search Results</title>\n";
Result+="<meta http-equiv='Content-Type' content='text/html; charset=UTF-8'>\n";
Result+='<script language="javascript" type="text/javascript" charset="UTF-8" src="dhtml_search.js"></script>\n';
Result+='<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="stylesheet.css">\n';
Result+="<style type='text/css'>\n";
Result+=".searchDetails {font-family:verdana; font-size:8pt; font-weight:bold}\n";
Result+=".searchResults {font-family:verdana; font-size:8pt}\n";
Result+="</style>\n";
Result+="</head>\n";
Result+="<body onload='javascript:document.SearchForm.SearchText.focus()'>\n";
Result+='<table class="searchDetails" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" width="100%">\n';
Result+='<tr><td>Enter a keyword or phrase</td></tr>';
Result+='<tr><td>';
Result+='<form name="SearchForm" action="javascript:search(document.SearchForm.SearchText.value)">';
if(SearchWord.length>=1){
   while(SearchWord.indexOf("<")>-1 || SearchWord.indexOf(">")>-1 || SearchWord.indexOf('"')>-1){
       SearchWord=SearchWord.replace("<","&lt;").replace(">","&gt;").replace('"',"&quot;");
   }
}
Result+='<input type="text" name="SearchText" size="25" value="' + SearchWord + '" />';
Result+='&nbsp;<input type="submit" value="&nbsp;Go&nbsp;"/></form>';
Result+='</td></tr></table>\n';

if(SearchWord.length>=1){
   SearchWord=SearchWord.toLowerCase();
   this.status="Searching, please wait...";
   Result+="<table border='0' cellpadding='5' class='searchResults' width='100%'>";
   for(j=0;j<PageCount;j++){
       k=Page[j].length-1;
       for(i=0;i<k;i++){
           WordPos=Page[j][i].toLowerCase().indexOf(SearchWord);
           if(WordPos>-1){
               FoundWord=Page[j][i].substr(WordPos,SearchWord.length);
               NrRes++;
               Result+="<tr><td>";
               Result+="<a target='BODY' href='"+Page[j][k]+"'>"+Page[j][k-1].replace(FoundWord,FoundWord.bold())+"</a><br/>\n";

               if(i<k-1){
                   if(Page[j][i].length>350){
                       Result+="..."+Page[j][i].substr(WordPos-100,200+FoundWord.length).replace(FoundWord,FoundWord.bold())+"...\n";
                   }
                   else{
                       Result+=Page[j][i].replace(FoundWord,FoundWord.bold())+"\n";
                   }
               }
               Result+="</td></tr>";
               break;
           }
       }
   }
   Result+="</table>";
   Result+="<p class='searchDetails'>&nbsp;" + NrRes + " result(s) found.</p>";
}

Result+="</body></html>";
this.status="";
this.document.open();
this.document.write(Result);
this.document.close();
}